WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.858
Two apples. Take three more. Give
one to Sami. What do you end up with?

2
00:00:09.078 --> 00:00:14.372
You can work that out before you do it.
And when a step is wrong, you can fix it.

3
00:00:14.872 --> 00:00:19.238
The output of an algorithm is what
you have when every step is done.

4
00:00:19.458 --> 00:00:24.938
Here is the algorithm. Take two apples.
Take three more. Give one to Sami.

5
00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:29.291
Say the output before you follow
the steps. That is predicting.

6
00:00:29.511 --> 00:00:33.365
Now follow every step with
your finger. Two apples.

7
00:00:33.585 --> 00:00:37.765
Take three more, and you have five.
Give one away, and you have four.

8
00:00:37.985 --> 00:00:42.908
The output is four apples. If you
predicted four, you were right.

9
00:00:43.128 --> 00:00:47.168
Try another. Start with five
counters, take two away, add four.

10
00:00:47.388 --> 00:00:50.453
Five, three, seven. The
output is seven counters.

11
00:00:50.953 --> 00:00:55.225
A bug is a step that is wrong, or
a right step in the wrong place.

12
00:00:55.445 --> 00:01:00.925
Here is a tea algorithm. Cup. A
sock in the cup. Hot water. Milk.

13
00:01:01.145 --> 00:01:06.207
A sock in the cup does not belong in
a tea algorithm. That step is the bug.

14
00:01:06.427 --> 00:01:10.421
Here is another one. The water is
poured before the cup is there.

15
00:01:10.641 --> 00:01:14.356
Pouring the water is right.
Doing it too early is the bug.

16
00:01:14.856 --> 00:01:18.989
Finding the bug is half the job.
Correcting it is the other half.

17
00:01:19.209 --> 00:01:23.993
Do not throw the algorithm away. The
rest of it already works. Edit it.

18
00:01:24.213 --> 00:01:28.485
To edit means to change what is
already there, and keep the rest.

19
00:01:28.705 --> 00:01:32.560
Swap the sock for a tea bag.
Everything else stays as it was.

20
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:37.006
Or move the early step to where
it belongs. The cup comes first.

21
00:01:37.226 --> 00:01:41.591
Then run the algorithm again
and check. A proper cup of tea.

22
00:01:42.091 --> 00:01:47.199
An algorithm is a precise set of
instructions. It leaves nothing to guess.

23
00:01:47.419 --> 00:01:51.274
Turn left at the shop is precise.
You know which way and where.

24
00:01:51.494 --> 00:01:56.045
Go somewhere is vague. Vague means
you are left guessing. Where?

25
00:01:56.265 --> 00:02:00.259
Take three steps forward is
precise. Walk a bit is vague.

26
00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:04.891
A vague instruction makes Robo
guess. And Robo guesses badly.

27
00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:09.988
A linear algorithm is a straight
line of steps, one after another.

28
00:02:10.208 --> 00:02:14.713
Open the tin. Food in the bowl.
Bowl on the floor. Call the cat.

29
00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:18.927
Each step does a job, and each
one has its place in the line.

30
00:02:19.147 --> 00:02:22.815
You cannot spoon food out of
a tin that is still closed.

31
00:02:23.035 --> 00:02:26.426
Follow it exactly, in a straight
line, and the cat is fed.

32
00:02:26.926 --> 00:02:30.641
You can predict the output of an
algorithm before you follow it.

33
00:02:30.861 --> 00:02:36.387
A bug is a wrong step, or a right
step too early. Find it, then edit it.

34
00:02:36.607 --> 00:02:41.112
A precise instruction leaves nothing
to guess, so Robo can follow it.
